WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Output encoding review starts with an inventory of contexts.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:19.400
Identify where application data is written into HTML, attributes, URLs, JavaScript, JSON, CSS, XML, CSV, logs, reports, emails, and generated documents.

3
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:29.820
For each location, ask whether the data is trusted, which context it enters, which encoder or safe API is used, and whether any escaping has been bypassed.

4
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.120
Template review is especially important because a single unsafe helper can affect many pages.

5
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:51.560
Testing should include special characters that reveal encoding mistakes: angle brackets, quotes, apostrophes, ampersands, slashes, backslashes, line breaks, equals signs, and characters meaningful to the destination format.

6
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.020
Unit tests can verify helpers and serializers.

7
00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:01.680
Security tests can verify that rendered output displays the data rather than interpreting it as markup or code.

8
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.620
Reviewing generated HTML can help confirm that the browser receives what the developer intended.

9
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.220
Avoid brittle deny-list assumptions.

10
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.320
Blocking one tag, one event handler, or one character rarely solves the broader context problem.

11
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:27.300
Prefer safe framework patterns, context-specific encoders, serializers, and allowlists for constrained values such as URL schemes or CSS tokens.

12
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.520
Browser-based checks can be useful, but they should support code review rather than replace it.

13
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:41.340
The strongest process combines context inventory, code review, targeted tests, and attention to places where default escaping has been disabled.
